 "capsule": "The Xiongnu were the great steppe confederation unified by Modu Chanyu around 209–208 BCE, whose cavalry armies dominated the Mongolian steppe and repeatedly forced the early Han emperors into tribute and marriage-alliance (heqin) treaties rather than open war. Under chanyus such as Laoshang and Junchen the confederation contested control of the Western Regions and Hexi Corridor with Han China through the 2nd and 1st centuries BCE, before internal succession disputes split the polity in 60–58 BCE and again fatally in 48 CE. This entry covers the main/Northern Xiongnu lineage's long rivalry with and periodic tributary status to Han China down to the mid-2nd century CE, distinct from the Han-vassal Southern Xiongnu state that broke away in 48 CE.",
 "citations": [
